  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simplified map of the brain’s circadian timing network. It shows that light enters through the eye and primarily resets the SCN, the central clock, while food and behavior influence clocks in peripheral tissues. Small clock icons placed in the SCN, hypothalamus, dorsal striatum, cerebellum, brainstem, and peripheral tissues indicate that circadian oscillators are distributed across many parts of the body rather than confined to one site.\n\nArrows emphasize communication between these clocks. The SCN appears to send hormonal and neural signals to peripheral tissues and also interacts with the hypothalamus. The hypothalamus is depicted as a hub linking with several other brain regions, including the dorsal striatum, cerebellum, and brainstem, suggesting bidirectional coordination among central and extra-SCN clocks. The dorsal striatum and cerebellum each connect back toward hypothalamic or broader brain circuits, and the brainstem is also integrated into this network.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that circadian regulation is a hierarchical but interconnected system: light strongly entrains the SCN, while feeding and behavior can entrain peripheral clocks, and multiple brain regions contain local clocks that communicate to coordinate daily physiology and behavior.",
